00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:07.330
I think, it's really important to build
a strong foundation of experiences.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:11.560
Many of you have come to the school with
some, or maybe you think enough of those.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:14.120
Others of you don't have
very many of them, but

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:17.130
I would try gaining experience
in different fields.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:19.640
Always, always network.

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:23.940
Everyday you want to do more networking,
and I don't mean on Facebook.

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:26.000
It's reaching out to people.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.530
I think, one of the very best
things about this school.

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:30.490
Is not in the programs,
or in the classrooms,

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:34.210
but it's in the networking you can do
around the rest of the university, and

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:37.645
with the entrepreneurs, and
CEOs coming and speaking to you.

00:19:37.645 --> 00:19:39.950
[COUGH] When you're looking for

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:43.360
an opportunity, my choice was to
try to get in on the ground floor

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:47.350
of a very rapidly growing company,
where I also thought it was well-managed.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:48.950
And the dream scenario is
where you're working for

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:51.530
someone you really think
you can learn from.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:53.840
It's hard to get all those
factors together, but

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.590
rapid growth is going to
create lots of opportunity,

00:19:56.590 --> 00:20:00.080
because to advance you don't have to
climb over the back of someone else.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:03.480
Rapid growth tends to attract
other really great executives,

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:05.060
and you'll strengthen your networks.

00:20:06.616 --> 00:20:09.190
I think, there will come a time,
probably, not certainly,

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:10.910
when you're going to
decide I want to swing for

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:14.870
the stands, I want to found a company,
I want to be a co-founder of a company.

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:16.510
I want to run my own show.

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:18.950
For those of you working in
the private sector, I believe,

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:21.471
that's also for entrepreneurs
working in the social sector.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:23.930
You don't have to do that right away.

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:30.050
There's always going to be a demand for
outstanding leadership.

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:33.120
And on the other hand,
I don't want to discourage you.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:34.760
If you happen to be
the next Mark Zuckerberg,

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:39.884
I'd like to meet you right after
[LAUGH] this conversation.

00:20:39.884 --> 00:20:45.020
And I think, across all these factors, one
of the most important things you can look

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:50.080
for is the culture of the place you're
going to to join, culture really matters.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.924
Culture has to do with values.

00:20:51.924 --> 00:20:56.601
And in my years in the Valley,
I've seen there's companies with really,

00:20:56.601 --> 00:21:00.480
cultures that appeal to me and
others that do not.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:02.400
I don't want to make
a judgment about these, but

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:03.794
just have you be tuned up for them.

00:21:03.794 --> 00:21:08.108
My partner Randy Komisar wrote a book,
he teaches over at the engineering school,

00:21:08.108 --> 00:21:09.720
called The Monk in the Riddle.

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:15.762
A great book for you to read about money,
and his start up venture, funerals.com,

00:21:15.762 --> 00:21:21.280
but he contrasts the difference
between mercenaries and missionaries.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:26.520
And the difference is
really very profound.

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:29.530
The mercenary cultures are quite driven,
and

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.780
paranoia is kind of at
the heart of what drives them.

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:34.890
I would frequently have
arguments with Andy Grove.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.723
Andy Grove says, only the paranoid
survive, and I'd say, hey,

00:21:38.723 --> 00:21:40.716
Andy paranoia is a disease state.

00:21:40.716 --> 00:21:44.206
That may not be the best way to motivate,
because instead of paranoia,

00:21:44.206 --> 00:21:47.020
you can have passion for
what you're doing.

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:48.760
Paranoia pushes you forward.

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:51.493
Passion draws you into an opportunity.

00:21:51.493 --> 00:21:55.969
It's the difference between opportunistic
and being strategic, or the difference

00:21:55.969 --> 00:22:00.195
between mercenaries and missionaries is
the difference between the pitch, and

00:22:00.195 --> 00:22:03.000
the deal performing a real partnership.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.770
This difference can be seen in
the time view that you have.

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:11.410
Is this venture on a sprint to go build
the next Instagram as fast as we can?

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:14.100
Or are we going to take a very long view?

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:17.360
The way Jeff does at Amazon.com.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:21.510
I'm not judging one, or another as right,
or wrong, but it's an important dimension.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:24.850
Are you going to obsess on the
competition, or obsess on the customers?

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:28.350
Are you going to set up the culture,
so there's a kind of aristocracy of

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:32.270
the founders, who really call all
the shot, or do you want a meritocracy

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:36.245
where you can get all the ideas on
the table, have the best ideas win.

00:22:36.245 --> 00:22:38.820
So, difference between focusing
on the financial statements, or

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.060
the value statements.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:42.580
I think, it's the difference
between whether, or not

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:46.510
you're in a culture where there's a lot of
loners, and you're on your own, or there's

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:52.110
a real emphasis on mentoring, on coaching,
so that the whole team gets better.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.820
I think, it's the difference
between an attitude of entitlement

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:57.730
which I find unappealing, or
the Hewlett-Packard idea,

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:01.350
that, people go there,
they make a contribution, it's recognized.

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:03.320
And that makes everybody more effective.

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:05.960
In Randy's book,

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:10.570
he calls it the difference between
the differed life plan, and a whole life.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:11.350
One that really works.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:16.800
My take on this, is it's a difference
between a lust for making money.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:19.850
And a lust for
making meaning as well as money.

00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:23.730
And any entrepreneur that comes to
tell us that they're not interested in

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:25.985
making money,
these are the enterprise, for

00:23:25.985 --> 00:23:29.250
profit entrepreneurs,
I usually don't believe them.

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:33.153
But, I think, you can achieve success and
significance,

00:23:33.153 --> 00:23:37.690
not just focus on success by choosing
what it is that you choose to do.

00:23:41.926 --> 00:23:43.390
Here's my last thought on this topic.

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:47.090
Anything that you want to do
of consequence, I believe,

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:49.360
you're going to do with others.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:54.960
And what this school is all
about is helping you grow and

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.330
be more capable as a leader.

00:23:57.330 --> 00:23:59.770
Ideas, I worship at the altar of ideas.

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:00.830
Innovation.

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:03.683
I'm a junky for it and
I'm sure you're attracted to it as well.

00:24:03.683 --> 00:24:09.041
But ideas are relatively speaking easy,
it's execution that's everything.

00:24:09.041 --> 00:24:12.860
The difference between San Mica systems
and Apollo was how they executed,

00:24:12.860 --> 00:24:17.430
the difference between Facebook and
MySpace was how they executed,

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:20.940
and it takes a team to execute well.

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:25.449
These three ideas, that ideas
are relatively easy, but important,

00:24:25.449 --> 00:24:27.752
execution is everything,
it takes a team to win,

00:24:27.752 --> 00:24:32.280
you want to figure out how to be a very
effective member of a high performance

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:37.010
team, and then lead a team of your
own to enable them and empower them.

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:40.585
And that I dare say is going to be the
most valuable thing you'll take from your

00:24:40.585 --> 00:24:42.707
