00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:56.700
I think we're going to have
time to come back for more.

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:03.492
So, I talked about the value of my
experience, anyway at Intel and

00:28:03.492 --> 00:28:08.307
then somebody asked me a really
tough question, which is,

00:28:08.307 --> 00:28:11.309
what would be my advice to an MBA now?

00:28:11.309 --> 00:28:16.098
And I think the place where I'd start
is to recommend that you go to a field,

00:28:16.098 --> 00:28:19.596
first that you believe is attractive,
but secondly,

00:28:19.596 --> 00:28:24.404
where you have technical expertise,
some kind of technology expertise.

00:28:24.404 --> 00:28:27.553
And that doesn't mean you're gotta
go get a degree in engineering or

00:28:27.553 --> 00:28:29.572
computer science, if you don't have one.

00:28:29.572 --> 00:28:34.390
But if I had decided that I was really
interested in technology of hybrid

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:37.762
seed breeding,
I would find while I'm here one or

00:28:37.762 --> 00:28:43.390
two courses outside of this school and
audit those or take those for credit.

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:46.610
Because you are also at one
of the great scientific and

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:48.586
engineering institutions of the world.

00:28:48.586 --> 00:28:52.190
And I'd work to network in that
way because I think innovation and

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:54.700
technology is a great disruptor.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:58.960
That doesn't mean you have to pursue
seed breeding as a career for

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:00.840
the rest of your life.

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:05.180
But I must tell you, all the CEOs
that I know, or startup executives

00:29:05.180 --> 00:29:10.150
who are trying to lead and run technology
companies, and who can't program or

00:29:10.150 --> 00:29:13.860
don't have some grounding in
the technology, they regret that.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:17.210
So that'd be one piece of advice.

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:21.470
The second thing is, I would,
as I said before, network like crazy.

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:24.416
I hate to say this with the dean here but

00:29:24.416 --> 00:29:27.480
I don't think your grades at
Stanford Business School matter.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.400
What matters is going to be,

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:35.397
of course, they're important,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:35.397 --> 00:29:37.805
&gt;&gt; But I think the quality of the networks

00:29:37.805 --> 00:29:41.946
and your hunger for learning and
figure out how you're going to make

00:29:41.946 --> 00:29:46.450
a difference in the world,
is I believe more important than that.

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:49.150
Is there any third advice?

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:52.390
Okay, so
this is out of the professional domain.

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:56.730
But I am convinced, without doubt,
and it may goes without saying,

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:59.740
that the most important decision
you'll make during, say your 20s,

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:03.650
is who it is that you would choose
to spend the rest of your life with.

00:30:03.650 --> 00:30:06.350
So don't give that short shift.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.540
Don't wait until you're 30 to
figure out who you may marry,

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:13.640
because you might find yourself
running out of time by that time and

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:17.190
end up choosing whoever it
is you happen to be with.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:20.210
There's a wonderful author who's
just put out a book on this topic

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:23.080
called the defining decade.

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:24.270
And her name is Meg Jay.

00:30:24.270 --> 00:30:26.480
And I won't say anything more about that.

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:29.740
But those would be three of
the top things in my agenda.

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:31.630
And price, the number jar,
I have that covered.

00:30:31.630 --> 00:30:36.700
So forgive me.

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:37.840
Let's go to the next big thing.

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:41.989
Enterprise opportunities in new spaces.

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:47.690
There's a revolution underway
that's almost as big as mobile.

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:49.860
And that's the move to Cloud Computing.

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:54.340
And enterprises has initially resisted
this, and now they're embracing it.

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:58.020
The estimates are that we're going to
go from zero cloud revenues,

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:02.020
which is when Amazon web services
started here, a few years ago,

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:06.490
we held a reception with Jeff Besos
to $270 billion by 2020.

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:14.020
Just services, that's an enormous
shift in how computing is done.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:18.290
So I think the Cloud fundamentally,
for economic reasons,

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:23.840
is going to transform how we compute
around the world, and that has

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:29.140
consequences in terms of the architecture
for Cloud Computing, the software, systems

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:35.320
are now being built to scale horizontally
in services as opposed to go vertically.

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:38.650
And the opportunities ahead are fantastic.

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:42.130
I have a couple, actually three
partners at Kleiner who focus on this.

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:45.514
Sorry, three and a half,
including Mike Abbott, who is

00:31:45.514 --> 00:31:50.255
the guy who went to Twitter two years ago
when the fail well was always coming up.

00:31:50.255 --> 00:31:53.220
Do you know what I mean, and
he did two remarkable things.

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:59.090
The most impressive was in 18 months time,
he personally recruited 300 top engineers

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:03.350
in the toughest recruiting market,
In the world, which is San Francisco.

00:32:03.350 --> 00:32:06.800
And then he organized those
engineers to stand up that service.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:09.350
So it's scaled as they've
gotten to 150 million,

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:12.470
even more users without going down.

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:15.608
We took a bet on him and
that's when we invested, when he arrived.

00:32:15.608 --> 00:32:20.760
And I think it illustrates the power
of those cloud technologies, and

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:26.600
also something else I said, which is,
the new currency is engineers.

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:28.800
They are what are very dear.

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:32.490
And I think that's true whether
it's in computer science, or

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:36.810
whether it's in life sciences, or
whether it's in energy innovation.

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:40.050
Which goes back to my
recommendation to you which is

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:43.300
don't be afraid of technology if your
not an engineer or a technologist.

00:32:43.300 --> 00:32:45.130
Dive in there, audit those courses.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:46.820
Figure out the publications
you want to read.

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:52.670
Bill Gates graduated from Harvard
without a degree at all and

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:56.230
he self taught himself to
be an expert in vaccines.

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:57.860
So you can do this.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:01.450
What are the new spaces?

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:04.630
The big wide open ones that
we're looking for anyway.

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:08.990
Well, I'm not going to tell you about all
those, but there are some we talk about

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:14.330
and every year we go off and we look out
three to five or more years in the future.

00:33:14.330 --> 00:33:17.130
Our partner Bill Joyce,
he`s even further than the rest of us,

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:19.120
he looks around corners.

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:24.030
But in each of the areas that we work in,
for example in health care technologies,

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:28.088
we think it is possible now to build
very targeted therapeutics with

00:33:28.088 --> 00:33:30.031
something we call a tiger team.

00:33:30.031 --> 00:33:33.930
Where our venture doesn't go through
the long, expensive clinical trials.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:37.990
But takes a technology,
right up to that point.

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:41.950
And then sells it in a very novel
way to large pharmaceutical company.

00:33:41.950 --> 00:33:42.960
That's exciting.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:45.360
So, as I said already is digital health.

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:49.630
In the area of energy innovations,
my particular passion is batteries.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:53.540
It sounds pedestrian, but
storage is the holy grail.

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:55.350
If we could, together,

00:33:55.350 --> 00:34:00.080
make a battery that had three times
the energy density of today's batteries,

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:04.140
that means it would weight one-third
as much and cost one-third as much.

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:05.260
And if that was the case,

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:08.970
electric vehicles without subsidy would
be cheaper than gasoline vehicles.

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:10.790
Think of that.

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:15.400
We backed four battery companies,
some of them looked promising.

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:17.010
I don’t know if any of
them are going to work.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:19.620
But it's show time, we're going to
figure out in the next year or two.

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:23.390
I think the biggest
opportunities though are in and

00:34:23.390 --> 00:34:26.650
around the digital technologies,
the mobile and the web.

00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:30.030
And you can just see hints of
what those transformation are.

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.720
When you hang around Google or
read the technology press.

00:34:33.930 --> 00:34:35.400
How many have been in a self driving car?

00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:39.320
It's an extraordinary experience,
it really is.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:40.524
And what that will mean for

00:34:40.524 --> 00:34:44.544
the productivity of people who are wasting
time in traffic, the fuel that's wasted.

00:34:44.544 --> 00:34:48.800
The dollars they have tied up in cars as
opposed to typing in on your smartphone

00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:52.478
you'd like it outside your apartment or
dorm room and it appears.

00:34:52.478 --> 00:34:57.895
The safety, 35,000 people in the United
States die in traffic accidents,

00:34:57.895 --> 00:34:59.870
the congestion on the roads.

00:35:01.040 --> 00:35:05.480
God bless Larry Page, he's got and
Sergey, he's got a great big vision.

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:10.360
We believe that these wearable devices,
whether they are smart bands or

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:15.244
Google Glasses or gathering more data,
watches are going to be quite big.

00:35:15.244 --> 00:35:18.020
There's going to be
a big opportunity there.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:22.070
In many cases I think they'll take
the place of the smartphone that you carry

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:23.950
around with you.

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:27.400
I'm particularly passionate
about innovations in education.

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:31.370
I think the second largest most
screwed up part of the American and

00:35:31.370 --> 00:35:33.750
the worldwide economy is public education.

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:38.310
And in particular in about 20%
of those schools in our country,

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:40.360
we are killing the kids.

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:43.930
They do literally design where
they're going to put prisons

00:35:43.930 --> 00:35:46.970
around the neighborhoods where
the academic performance is terrible.

00:35:48.568 --> 00:35:51.760
Bill Gates says every life
ought to be created equal,

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:55.200
I think every kid ought to have
an opportunity to a great education.

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:58.424
I've been working in this field,
with Stanford, GSB,

00:35:58.424 --> 00:36:03.750
Ed School Alumnus Kim Smith,
to create a venture

00:36:03.750 --> 00:36:08.280
fund called the New Schools Venture Fund
and encourage entrepreneurs and fund them.

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:13.040
I think only now, can we begin to see
something that will scale very quickly.

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:17.077
You see it in Khan Academy, which has
over 5 million daily users taking their

00:36:17.077 --> 00:36:20.260
courses, and we know by studies,
advancing a lot further.

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:24.536
You see it in Coursera, right,
Coursera is 11 months old.

00:36:24.536 --> 00:36:28.997
They now have 2 and a half million
students who've signed up for 3 and

00:36:28.997 --> 00:36:32.852
a half million courses,
that come from 58 universities,

00:36:32.852 --> 00:36:37.488
with a course catalog of I think
it's something like 300 offerings.

00:36:37.488 --> 00:36:41.750
And they have good competition
also in this field.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:46.020
So finally, one of the last
bastions resistant to change,

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:50.890
the education system, the ones dominated
by teachers unions or higher ed,

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:54.030
which has the benefits of
tenure are extraordinary.

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:57.570
I remember when John Hennessey
called me about Coursera and I said,

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:59.110
so John, why are you excited about this?

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:02.770
And he said, you know what, the community
college model in the United States is

00:37:02.770 --> 00:37:05.690
broken, their business model is broken.

00:37:05.690 --> 00:37:09.790
The can't get enough really great faculty,
they can't get great curricula.

00:37:09.790 --> 00:37:13.466
But if you can take the mainstream or
even the specialty courses and

00:37:13.466 --> 00:37:17.218
offer that to US community colleges,
that's a great deal of good.

00:37:17.218 --> 00:37:21.130
And I haven't said anything
about education globally either.

00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:24.990
Okay, payments and NFC.

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:28.150
I confess I should know
more about this than I do.

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:34.510
I think there's very little doubt,
we can all agree that these devices,

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:38.280
this Android, which has NFC in it,
are going to become our mobile wallet.

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:39.150
These are amazing things.

00:37:39.150 --> 00:37:41.320
They're broadband, they're connected,
they're always on.

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:43.400
They know who you are,
they know where you are.

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:47.510
It's your diary, it's your entertainment,
it's your wallet, it's your identity.

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:52.650
But I remember talking to Steve about
this from the point of view of Apple.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:57.030
He just didn't believe in NFC, and
he didn't actually believe in trying to

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:00.110
create a digital wallet until
you could put everything in it.

00:38:00.110 --> 00:38:02.820
Your driver's licence,
your social security cards, so

00:38:02.820 --> 00:38:05.450
payments wasn't enough for him.

00:38:05.450 --> 00:38:08.480
Fortunately I'm really proud we're
able to back Jack Dorsey and

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:12.220
his company Square, where my
partner Mary Micker's on the board.

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:16.726
And they took a particular part of
the unserved payment system which is very

00:38:16.726 --> 00:38:17.881
small merchants.

00:38:17.881 --> 00:38:22.318
Said we're going to sweep through there
with an innovation, and then partner with

00:38:22.318 --> 00:38:26.870
large retailers like Starbucks to
transform the purchase experience.

00:38:26.870 --> 00:38:29.320
So how many of you use
Square on a smartphone?

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:33.510
Okay, so maybe 30% of us.

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:36.640
Where on your smartphone,
you walk into Starbucks, and

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:38.970
you just tell them what you want and
it's never rung up.

00:38:38.970 --> 00:38:42.650
It identifies who you are,
it concludes the transaction,

00:38:42.650 --> 00:38:46.410
it gives the retailer the history
of what you've bought before.

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:48.431
And I think that will spread.

00:38:48.431 --> 00:38:51.670
Yep, it will spread rapidly but
not explosively.

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:53.670
I think change of that
behavior takes time.

00:38:55.910 --> 00:39:00.010
Mobile payments is a really big deal and
probably there will be multiple winners.

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:02.860
Let's go to venture capital for a moment.

00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:09.040
This notion about, it takes $25 million
to start a VC fund, as I said before,

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:14.090
I think it takes $25 million to train
a good Venture Capitalist, which

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:18.160
is to say that the business that we work
in is one with risk and their failures.

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:20.020
Most ventures fail.

00:39:20.020 --> 00:39:24.510
As measured by they're not in business
five years after you invest in them.

00:39:24.510 --> 00:39:29.690
So, at least for my partnership, the way
we work is we try to find, in any given

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:36.730
fund, several ventures that will deliver
ten times, or 30 times, or 50 times.

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:42.470
And those will cover for
the losses of other ventures.

00:39:42.470 --> 00:39:44.150
And you live in an extraordinary place.

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:47.910
This is the only place in the world
that I know where it's okay to fail,

00:39:47.910 --> 00:39:50.990
and you can change your job without
even changing where you park your car.

00:39:50.990 --> 00:39:55.120
I mean it's very, very special to
the culture and economy of this place.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:40:01.640
The long feedback cycles
Are incredibly frustrating.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:06.320
What I will say, the long feedback cycles
in venture capital, in backing a team.

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:10.560
What I will tell you, is along the way
you can measure project progress.

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:13.220
Any growth says if you don't
measure it doesn't matter and

00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:15.400
I'm definitely of that religion.

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:20.770
You can have ventures that will lay
out goals by audience, by revenue,

00:40:20.770 --> 00:40:25.680
by technical progress, and talk to
your partners about how that's going.

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:31.170
At Kleiner, every quarter,
we have very detailed operational review.

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.650
Sometimes there's not much to it because
we're trying to build a breakthrough

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:41.020
battery, but other time it's
what's the audience now for Path?

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:43.580
How engaged are the Path
users in using this service?

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:48.140
What is their engagement rate?

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:50.940
How do the cohorts stay engaged or not?

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:54.530
So, I think lots of things about
the business can be measured, but

00:40:54.530 --> 00:40:58.070
the average to a return, they call
them exits, I don't like that word,

00:40:58.070 --> 00:41:02.710
but to liquidity is going to be on
the order of five, four, six years.

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:06.850
It was five years after we invested in
Google that it became a public company.

00:41:06.850 --> 00:41:11.526
It'll be 13 years after we invested in
Bloom Energy that it'll be a bigger than

00:41:11.526 --> 00:41:14.229
billion dollar profitable energy provider.

00:41:18.258 --> 00:41:20.300
Somebody asked who my favorite CEOs were.

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:24.020
And without ranking them in any
particular order, of course,

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.560
I think the one we all
miss the most is Steve.

00:41:26.560 --> 00:41:31.370
But Jeff Bezos is extraordinary for
the clarity of his vision and

00:41:31.370 --> 00:41:35.140
his commitment to the long term and his
willingness to experiment and innovate.

00:41:36.490 --> 00:41:40.030
I'm a big fan, as you can imagine,
of Larry and Sergei and Erik Schmitt.

00:41:40.030 --> 00:41:45.160
What those three guys did, when they
welcome, or the two founders did when they

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:50.060
welcomed Eric into their team, in
the next decade they built from nothing,

00:41:50.060 --> 00:41:53.910
a quarter billion dollars of
value in technology company.

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:58.370
And through that period of time our hope
was that Larry would be ready to take over

00:41:58.370 --> 00:42:01.032
from Eric,
which tuned out to be the case, so,

00:42:01.032 --> 00:42:03.379
great team, great CEO as their founder.

00:42:06.003 --> 00:42:09.020
I really admire Jack Dorsey.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:13.250
Here's the guy who was
the founder of Twitter and

00:42:13.250 --> 00:42:15.690
the founder of Square,
incredible sense of taste.

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:22.160
Some people say that Elon Musk is
going to be the next great CEO.

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:24.140
It's extraordinary what he's done, right?

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:26.560
He founded a successful
solar company that's public,

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:31.490
a successful car company that is public,
those are two great big green wins, and

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:33.090
along his way, built a spaceship company.

00:42:33.090 --> 00:42:38.710
[LAUGH] So, there's something going
on there that's quite important.

00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:40.810
Who else?

00:42:40.810 --> 00:42:43.380
Who else thinks there's a great
CEO out there who you admire?

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:45.070
Just put your hand up and call it out.

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:48.118
We're not going to get any takers here?

00:42:48.118 --> 00:42:49.055
Come on, yeah.

00:42:49.055 --> 00:42:52.620
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Pat Romano, okay.

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:53.120
Any others?

00:42:57.679 --> 00:42:58.573
Yep.

00:42:58.573 --> 00:43:00.780
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Marc Benioff.

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:01.810
&gt;&gt; I think Marc's fantastic.

00:43:02.870 --> 00:43:09.340
Among the new generation of CEO's, I'm a
huge fan of Mike McHugh over at Flipboard,

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:14.290
and Dave Mooren, he's one of the younger
CEO's that we backed at path.

00:43:14.290 --> 00:43:16.379
I'd watch these companies and
I think they're going to do very well.

00:43:16.379 --> 00:43:21.163
Hey, Tony Fadell, who built Nest Company
and did the iPod before that.

00:43:25.808 --> 00:43:28.530
Favorite CEOs, disruptors.

00:43:28.530 --> 00:43:33.550
What do you do if you're going to be in a
venture that's got saturated competition?

00:43:34.870 --> 00:43:36.490
Lots of good entrenched competitors,

00:43:36.490 --> 00:43:39.910
do you then obsess on the competition
instead of on the customer?

00:43:41.270 --> 00:43:42.130
I'd suggest no.

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:45.130
I'd suggest you do something else,
you know.

00:43:45.130 --> 00:43:50.600
The best way to build a great company is
to find a large underserved market or

00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:53.230
a displacement market that exists.

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:56.590
So, now if the Google guys followed my
advice, they wouldn't have built yet

00:43:56.590 --> 00:43:57.850
another search engine.

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:01.820
Here's the point, they had a really
disruptive technology, the page rank

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:05.690
system, that they were running out of
their dorm room at Stanford, and it was

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:09.310
growing so rapidly that Stanford decided
they were going to throw them off campus.

00:44:09.310 --> 00:44:10.960
It was a real load on the network, so

00:44:10.960 --> 00:44:16.450
they went down University Avenue to
the office above the ice cream parlor, and

00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:21.090
the rest is history, but
I really think there's wisdom in this.

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:25.225
Jeff Bezos says this, I'm going to obsess
on my customers, not my competition,

00:44:25.225 --> 00:44:29.292
because if I serve those customers better
than the competitors, I'm going to win.

00:44:29.292 --> 00:44:32.400
Jeff's a very disciplined
in his communications.

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:35.060
You'll notice the only words
that come out of this mouth,

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:38.800
the only thing you'll find in an Amazon
press release are customer benefits.

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:43.680
If it doesn't convey a customer benefit,
don't say it, that's all that matters.

00:44:43.680 --> 00:44:48.364
I remember when I first met Jeff,
he was going to build the world's

00:44:48.364 --> 00:44:53.080
most customer-centric company,
that was his goal.

00:44:53.080 --> 00:44:54.800
And I think to a large measure,
he's achieved it.

00:44:57.290 --> 00:44:58.010
Payments.

00:44:58.010 --> 00:45:01.000
Natural gas, a word or
two about natural gas.

00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:04.430
This is a big disruptor,
nobody forecasted this.

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:08.610
And if I can be a red-blooded
patriotic American citizen right now,

00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:10.160
it's a very good thing for our economy.

00:45:10.160 --> 00:45:12.800
I think it's going to mean
manufacturing comes back.

00:45:12.800 --> 00:45:14.790
It's a very good thing for our climate.

00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:18.350
The greenhouse gas emissions
from an equivalent unit of

00:45:18.350 --> 00:45:21.100
energy are about half of what
they would be from coal,

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:24.170
which means we aren't building
new coal fired powerplants.

00:45:24.170 --> 00:45:26.920
It advantages some of
the investments we've made and

00:45:26.920 --> 00:45:28.640
disadvantages others of them.

00:45:28.640 --> 00:45:32.440
You'll be happy to know we haven't
invested in any coal fired powerplants but

00:45:32.440 --> 00:45:36.680
clean energy, great advantage in
having cheap, reliable natural gas for

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:38.970
a long period of time.

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:41.630
What's the role for venture capital
in these kinds of innovations?

00:45:43.050 --> 00:45:44.940
Look, we've learned some
lessons along the way.

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:47.600
We started investing in green
energy in 2007, I think,

00:45:47.600 --> 00:45:52.770
and some of the projects
were capital intensive.

00:45:52.770 --> 00:45:58.610
And of those projects, some of them I
wish we didn't do, we made mistakes.

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:01.330
But others that are capital
intensive like Bloom Energy,

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:02.900
I think they're going to be huge winners.

00:46:02.900 --> 00:46:04.660
So you remember that slide
I put up at the end?

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:08.110
Ideas are easy, execution is everything,
it takes teams to win.

00:46:08.110 --> 00:46:12.400
A great difference between the successful
ventures, and the not successful ones

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:17.470
in green, whether or not they are capital
intensive, is how they execute.

00:46:17.470 --> 00:46:22.470
Elan Musk executed with Tesla incredibly
well in a capital intensive car company.

00:46:22.470 --> 00:46:25.660
It's worth $4 billion today.

00:46:25.660 --> 00:46:29.110
So, execution is everything.

00:46:29.110 --> 00:46:31.840
Nonetheless, I think if you're going to
invest as a venture capitalist in this

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:36.910
field, you better pick the low capital
projects and you better build the broad

00:46:36.910 --> 00:46:41.720
base of investors and get strategic
alliances to help get to the market.

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:46.250
These are incredibly large markets,
it just takes time to get there,

00:46:46.250 --> 00:46:47.040
time and smarts.

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:53.580
This is the personal one,
that was mostly hot stock tips and,

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:56.170
so the question I think
was up there is so, John,

00:46:56.170 --> 00:46:58.480
what's your financial forecast for
sectors of the economy?

00:46:58.480 --> 00:47:02.380
I don't know, that's, I'm not
going to be able to help with that.

00:47:02.380 --> 00:47:06.550
The dinner with the president, what was
the advice to the president that came.

00:47:06.550 --> 00:47:10.330
Not so much for me, but all the executives
who were around the table who included

00:47:10.330 --> 00:47:14.560
Carol Barts and Larry Schmidt and
Mark Zuckerberg and Steve Jobs.

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:18.555
We put Mark on the right of Steve,
I asked him to not wear sandals [LAUGH].

00:47:19.590 --> 00:47:23.820
And on his left was Steve, and
it was a very free ranging conversation

00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:27.400
with an incredibly bright
leader of the country.

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:31.090
As I recall, Art Levinson argued for
changing the regiment for

00:47:31.090 --> 00:47:35.520
approving drugs based on the new molecular
understanding that we have of diseases,

00:47:35.520 --> 00:47:36.860
with the FDA having slowed up.

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:41.410
Let's see, Reed Hastings made
some impassioned arguments in

00:47:41.410 --> 00:47:42.680
favor of public education.

00:47:45.130 --> 00:47:46.030
Larry Ellison.

00:47:46.030 --> 00:47:48.330
He went on a real terror
around immigration.

00:47:48.330 --> 00:47:50.100
This whole idea that we have a staple and

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:54.360
green card to the Diplomas of foreign
nationals who were educated and

00:47:54.360 --> 00:47:59.220
the difficulty, the craziness that we'll
have foreign nationals come to our great

00:47:59.220 --> 00:48:01.580
universities and
then tell them they have to leave, I mean.

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:04.040
What kind of national strategy is that?

00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:10.640
So I think there were themes that you
would know about and resonate with.

00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:15.400
What was impressive is that he came
here to learn about technology and

00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:17.320
not to do any fundraising.

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:20.680
He came for dinner and
then went up to the Pacific northwest.

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:24.200
The Jobs Act is something that came
out of the President's Jobs Council.

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:27.840
And Steve Case deserves enormous credit
for driving that through Congress in

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:30.940
a bipartisan way in a Congress
that really didn't do anything.

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:33.460
There's three components of that.

00:48:33.460 --> 00:48:36.930
The first that I really cared about
was reforms and how you go public.

00:48:36.930 --> 00:48:41.280
So you're not subject to the withering
criticism that GroupOn, for

00:48:41.280 --> 00:48:42.934
example, had to endure.

00:48:42.934 --> 00:48:46.434
Now you can file,
not have all that information be public,

00:48:46.434 --> 00:48:50.710
until you decide you're really
going to go out for an offering.

00:48:50.710 --> 00:48:55.821
They also changed some of
the over reaches, I believe,

00:48:55.821 --> 00:49:01.060
from the Spitzer era around disclosure and
analysts.

00:49:01.060 --> 00:49:04.210
There's a couple other
parts of the Jobs Act that

00:49:04.210 --> 00:49:05.520
it's going to remain to see what happens.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:08.400
There's people who are predicting
there'll be a lot of abuses

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:12.520
when any entrepreneur can raise $1
million off the web, essentially.

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:13.810
But here's what I believe.

00:49:13.810 --> 00:49:15.710
Our country ought to
have more entrepreneurs.

00:49:15.710 --> 00:49:19.710
We ought to have more capital that can
get to entrepreneurs and more innovation.

00:49:19.710 --> 00:49:22.040
So I'm glad this act was passed, and

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:25.820
I think any corrections that need to be
made can be done on a regulatory basis.

00:49:27.470 --> 00:49:29.750
We're coming quickly to
the close of our time, so

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:33.130
I think what I'd like to do is ask for
three more questions.

00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:37.850
Just those three, and I'll try to
answer them right after you pose them.

00:49:37.850 --> 00:49:38.934
You've got a mic there, thank you.

00:49:38.934 --> 00:49:39.780
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name's Josiah.

00:49:39.780 --> 00:49:40.910
I'm a second year MBA.

00:49:40.910 --> 00:49:44.203
You mentioned and you showed us the growth
of smartphones and then tablets.

00:49:44.203 --> 00:49:47.754
I'd be interested in your thoughts on what
you think the next screen's going to be

00:49:47.754 --> 00:49:50.722
and how far behind we are in terms
of that next growth trajectory for

00:49:50.722 --> 00:49:53.914
that specific screen,
whether it's home television, Google Apps.

00:49:53.914 --> 00:49:56.462
&gt;&gt; So I think the next great screen
is going to be the second screen in

00:49:56.462 --> 00:49:58.010
the living room.

00:49:58.010 --> 00:50:02.072
And Apple does not yet
own the living room, neither does Comcast.

00:50:02.072 --> 00:50:04.770
We batch entrepreneurs who
hope to build services in

00:50:04.770 --> 00:50:06.570
advance of those screens coming out.

00:50:06.570 --> 00:50:07.970
Maybe they're going to be Android tablets.

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:12.340
I don't know, but I think that's
the most important next screen.

00:50:12.340 --> 00:50:14.829
If your question was about
the screen display itself,

00:50:14.829 --> 00:50:17.805
there's this company called
Luxview that's figured out a way,

00:50:17.805 --> 00:50:21.070
did you know 90% of the power that
goes into a screen today is wasted?

00:50:22.160 --> 00:50:27.080
There's this backlit emitting device, and
then it goes through all kinds of filters.

00:50:27.080 --> 00:50:30.890
Well, if instead, you have a technology
that can put down tiny LEDs,

00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:35.180
two of them per pixel, and they directly
emit the light, you get ten times

00:50:35.180 --> 00:50:39.110
greater brightness out of that device,
and it's flexible, it can fold.

00:50:39.110 --> 00:50:40.720
So you can see it vividly out of doors.

00:50:40.720 --> 00:50:44.260
That's what Luxview is making and
building, and

00:50:44.260 --> 00:50:48.320
it'll transform the biggest source
of power consumption in the devices.

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:51.240
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; Hi John, I'm Lance,

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:55.250
I'm a PhD student here, and I'm curious to
know what you guys aren't interested in.

00:50:55.250 --> 00:50:57.799
Either because you think
there's been over investment or

00:50:57.799 --> 00:51:00.045
because the impact of
technology's overstated.

00:51:00.045 --> 00:51:04.677
&gt;&gt; Hm, that's a good question.

00:51:07.972 --> 00:51:12.641
We're not interested in the next
relatively inconsequential anonymous

00:51:12.641 --> 00:51:17.383
social dating site that will allow you
to get laid before you get to the end of

00:51:17.383 --> 00:51:18.218
Palm Drive.

00:51:18.218 --> 00:51:22.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We've ruled those out.

00:51:25.237 --> 00:51:26.680
We can't do everything.

00:51:26.680 --> 00:51:31.140
So there are some venture firms
that are focusing on, for

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:34.065
example, retail technologies.

00:51:34.065 --> 00:51:39.110
There are venture firms that are investing
broadly in diagnostic devices,

00:51:39.110 --> 00:51:43.000
which we did at a period of time,
and we've stopped investing it.

00:51:46.160 --> 00:51:50.565
We're generally not investing
in new hardware, but

00:51:50.565 --> 00:51:51.910
NIST would be a counter-example.

00:51:51.910 --> 00:51:55.790
Whatever rules I tell you,
I want to tell you are just guidelines.

00:51:55.790 --> 00:51:57.777
The key to my business is, every year,

00:51:57.777 --> 00:52:01.070
we receive 3,000 proposals
from entrepreneurs.

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:02.330
We read them all.

00:52:02.330 --> 00:52:06.105
We take probably 500 meetings with
entrepreneurs out of those 3,000.

00:52:06.105 --> 00:52:07.957
And the first thing we read, by the way,

00:52:07.957 --> 00:52:10.368
is the back of the proposal
to look at their resumes.

00:52:10.368 --> 00:52:14.828
We take 500 first meetings,
those result in 100 second meetings, and

00:52:14.828 --> 00:52:17.915
in the end,
we'll make 30 new investments per year

00:52:17.915 --> 00:52:20.806
over those three groups
that I'm talking about.

00:52:20.806 --> 00:52:23.242
So we're pretty picky.

00:52:23.242 --> 00:52:25.890
We measure ourselves on how
many of the investments we see

00:52:25.890 --> 00:52:27.910
that are competitors at May.

00:52:27.910 --> 00:52:29.430
We do a six month retrospective.

00:52:29.430 --> 00:52:32.310
There's a half dozen competitors
we pay attention to.

00:52:32.310 --> 00:52:36.539
We are seeing right around 50% in
the investments our competitors make, and

00:52:36.539 --> 00:52:39.013
our goal is to drive
that to greater than 60.

00:52:39.013 --> 00:52:41.630
It's been improving about 10% a year.

00:52:41.630 --> 00:52:43.900
Then we measure how often we
think we make the right decision.

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:47.133
I'm not going to reveal that number.

00:52:47.133 --> 00:52:50.291
[LAUGH] But I will tell you when we
decide to compete for an investment,

00:52:50.291 --> 00:52:52.479
we measure that, and
we win 80% of the time.

00:52:52.479 --> 00:52:54.500
So we're not trying to
work on that ratio so

00:52:54.500 --> 00:52:56.700
much as a coverage in
better decision making.

00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:00.310
I'd like to close with two thoughts.

00:53:00.310 --> 00:53:03.390
The first is that, for
those of you who would some day like to

00:53:03.390 --> 00:53:08.040
be in the venture capital business,
my advice is get really meaningful,

00:53:08.040 --> 00:53:10.440
successful entrepreneurial
experience first.

00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:14.819
I really recommend that because it's
going to help you serve the entrepreneurs

00:53:14.819 --> 00:53:16.174
you're working with.

00:53:16.174 --> 00:53:18.735
It's going to help your judgement,

00:53:18.735 --> 00:53:23.780
it's going to help you compete to
win the opportunity to advise them.

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:26.247
You can't buy a position
on the board of directors,

00:53:26.247 --> 00:53:28.274
you want to earn
the opportunity to do that.

00:53:28.274 --> 00:53:31.834
because you can hopefully add some value
to what the entrepreneurs are going to do,

00:53:31.834 --> 00:53:32.557
and together,

00:53:32.557 --> 00:53:36.510
you can make new mistakes instead of
affecting the mistakes of the past.

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:39.750
My second thought is there's never
been a better time than now to

00:53:39.750 --> 00:53:42.730
start a new company or
to be part of a startup.

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:47.274
It may or may not work in your career,
but this explosion of technology that I

00:53:47.274 --> 00:53:51.818
described, the fact that the market of
Internet users is a billion people,

00:53:51.818 --> 00:53:53.178
I kind of pinch myself.

00:53:53.178 --> 00:53:57.823
I can't believe I'm alive at this time,
working in this place,

00:53:57.823 --> 00:54:02.000
with the really great
opportunities that we have.

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:03.370
So here's the deal.

00:54:03.370 --> 00:54:07.745
If you would like a copy of these slides,
I'd like you to send me a mail message to

00:54:07.745 --> 00:54:14.770
Jdoor@kpcb.com with your
three favorite books.

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:19.010
And I'll send you the slides and
three of my favorite books.

00:54:19.010 --> 00:54:21.790
Before I sign off, I want to tell you
three that I've read recently and

00:54:21.790 --> 00:54:24.510
that I'm enjoying a lot.

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:28.775
The first is Al Gore's The Future,
a very unpretentious name,

00:54:28.775 --> 00:54:33.170
[LAUGH] where he's describing
six global drivers of change.

00:54:33.170 --> 00:54:36.760
And it's really worth reading,
maybe a chapter at a time.

00:54:36.760 --> 00:54:41.544
Second is this book I already mentioned
called The Defining Decade by Meg Jay.

00:54:41.544 --> 00:54:45.352
It's not written for me,
it's written for 20-somethings.

00:54:45.352 --> 00:54:48.333
And then the third, if I can promote it,

00:54:48.333 --> 00:54:53.335
is Cheryl Sandberg's book that just
came out today called Lean In,

00:54:53.335 --> 00:54:57.125
which is about women,
work, and the will to lead.

00:54:57.125 --> 00:54:58.551
Thank you very much for the time.

00:54:58.551 --> 00:55:00.298
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I look forward to hearing from you.

00:55:00.298 --> 00:55:10.499
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:55:10.499 --> 00:55:11.050
[MUSIC]

